<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.8.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00504</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.8.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>81a9049e-b9de-4580-8dee-4d5fcb2f087c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For questions 1 – 2, use the following passage:</para>
<para><quote>A runner is training for a marathon. She runs more than 4 days each week. Each day, she runs between 12 and 15 miles. The marathon is 3 months away at the most.</quote></para><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_0ca15f01-7b3c-49e2-a528-491f804852e2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> What does the inequality \(d &gt; 4\) represent? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>the number of miles in a marathon</para><para>the number of miles the runner runs each day</para><para>the number of months away the marathon is</para><para>the number of days the runner runs each week</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! the number of days the runner runs each week</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3.</para><para>The correct answer is the number of days the runner runs each week. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5de3c9b4-ffc9-49f9-86b6-f512ceaeb3bb" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> What does the inequality \(12\;\leq\;m\;\leq\;15\) represent? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>the number of miles in a marathon</para><para>the number of miles the runner runs each day</para><para>the number of months away the marathon is</para><para>the number of days the runner runs each week</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! the number of miles the runner runs each day</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is the number of miles the runner runs each day. </para></solution></exercise></section><para>For 3 - 8, write an inequality to represent the constraint given. </para>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_04452122-a496-4774-88f1-f37f209b48b8" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> There are less than 4 patients, \(p\), in the waiting room. </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(p &lt; 4 \)</para><para>\(p &gt; 4 \)</para><para>\(p\;\leq\;4\;\)</para><para>\(p\;\geq\;4\;\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(p\;&lt;\;4\;\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(p\;&lt;\;4\;\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="a25872c8-fc00-4da6-af27-9ea7337fd151" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> There are no more than 6 cats, \(c\), in the yard. </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(c &gt; 6 \)</para><para>\(c\;\leq\;6 \)</para><para>\(c &lt; 6 \)</para><para>\(c\;\geq\;6\;\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \( c ≤ 6 \)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(c≤6\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_3f993c8d-6884-4bbb-8604-aa21aba0598c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> There are no less than 7 markers, \(m\), in the drawer. </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(m &gt; 7 \)</para><para>\(m\;\leq\;7\)</para><para>\(m &lt; 7\)</para><para>\(m\;\geq\;7\;\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(m\;\geq\;7\;\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(m\;\geq\;7\;\). </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_21948567-9795-45ab-ba5c-d269cd4e7474" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> The basket has between 12 and 19 eggs, \(e\). </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(12 &gt; e\) and \(\;e\;\;&gt;\;19\;\)</para><para>\(12\;\geq\;e\;\)and\(\;e\;\geq\;19\;\)</para><para>\(e\;\leq\;19\;\)</para><para>\(12\;≤\;e\;≤\;19\;\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(12 ≤ e ≤ 19 \)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(12 ≤ e ≤ 19\). </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_57a2b48e-e646-40cd-a130-2bda02cb8b94" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> The total sales of tickets to a show is more than $68. An adult ticket, <emphasis effect="italics">a</emphasis>, costs $8 and a children’s ticket, \(c\), is $3. </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(8a\;+\;3c\;\leq\;68\;\)</para><para>\(8a\;+\;3c\;\geq\;68\;\)</para><para>\(8a + 3c &lt; 68 \)</para><para>\(8a + 3c &gt; 68 \)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(8a + 3c &gt; 68 \)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(8a + 3c &gt; 68\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_40ce7a53-87d6-4d8c-9da5-f411619fb487" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> There needs to be at least 1 pound of clay, \(c\), for every 4 students, \(s\). </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(c\;\geq\;\frac s4\;\)</para><para>\(c\;&gt;\;\frac s4\;\)</para><para>\(c\;&lt;\;\frac s4\;\)</para><para>\(c\;\leq\;\frac s4\;\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(c\;\geq\;\frac s4\;\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(p\;\geq\;\frac s4\;\). </para></solution></exercise></section><para>For 9 - 11, use the following passage: </para>
<para><quote>A doctor sees between 7 and 12 patients each day. On Mondays and Tuesdays, the
    appointment times are 15 minutes. On Wednesdays and Thursdays, they are 30 minutes long. On Fridays, they are one hour long. The doctor works for no more than 8 hours a day. Here are some inequalities that represent this situation. </quote></para><para>\(0.25\;\leq\;y\;\leq\;1\)            \(7\;\leq\;x\;\leq\;12\)           \(xy\;\leq\;8\)</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_80586fbc-b2d8-421d-acc7-0fadf313b894" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> What does the variable <emphasis effect="italics">x</emphasis> represent? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>the number of patients the doctor sees each day.</para><para>the part of an hour that each appointment lasts.</para><para>the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day.</para><para>the number of patients the doctor sees in a week.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! the number of patients the doctor sees each day</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.2. </para><para>The correct answer is the number of patients the doctor sees each day. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c8c68be2-5987-4311-bc26-ec6a58774d65" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> What does the variable <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis> represent? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>the number of patients the doctor sees each day.</para><para>the part of an hour that each appointment lasts.</para><para>the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day.</para><para>the number of patients the doctor sees in a week.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! the part of an hour that each appointment lasts</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.2. </para><para>The correct answer is the part of an hour that each appointment lasts. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_52e5dd2c-2137-4963-ad5b-91a7f20a4320" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> What does the expression \(xy\) in the last inequality mean in this situation? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>the number of patients the doctor sees each day.</para><para>the part of an hour that each appointment lasts.</para><para>the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day.</para><para>the number of patients the doctor sees in a week.</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.2. </para><para>The correct answer is the total time the doctor spends working with patients each day. </para></solution></exercise></section><para>For 12 - 14, use the following passage:</para>
<para><quote>Arnav wants to build a dog house. He makes a list of the materials needed:</quote><quote>Arnav's budget is $65. Plywood costs $0.70 per square foot, planks of wood cost $0.10 per foot, and paint costs $8 per quart.</quote></para><section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="bc2297ed-28d8-428f-b938-3a65cf880bf6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Which inequality represents the square feet of plywood needed? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(p\;\geq\;60\)</para><para>\(p\;\geq\;36\)</para><para>\(1\;\leq\;p\;\leq\;2\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(p\;\geq\;60\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(p\;\geq\;60\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="ab779033-04ec-4745-95d6-404d192cfdf3" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Which inequality represents the number of wood planks needed? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(w\;\geq\;60\)</para><para>\(1\;\leq\;w\;\leq\;2\)</para><para>\(w\;\geq\;36\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(w\;\geq\;36\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(w\;\geq\;36\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="b2f185f1-5900-4c03-b982-b2d373c39ad9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Which inequality represents the number of quarts of paint needed? </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(q\;\geq\;60\)</para><para>\(1\;\leq\;q\;\leq\;2\)</para><para>\(q\;\geq\;36\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(1\;\leq\;q\;\leq\;2\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.8.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(1\;\leq\;q\;\leq\;2\). </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
